  Rayleigh quotient of that eigenvector, LIFTED periodically (exact identity at any even L)
    L=2: RQ = -10.000000   (lambda_min = -9.999998)
    L=4: RQ = -10.000000
    L=6: RQ = -10.000000
    L=8: RQ = -10.000000

  naive 'uniform on the direction-1 links' modes (corpus description) -- RQ:
    L=2: RQ(dir1, gen1..3) = +0.0000 +0.0000 +0.0000   RQ(all links, gen3) = +0.0000
    L=4: RQ(dir1, gen1..3) = +0.0000 +0.0000 +0.0000   RQ(all links, gen3) = -0.0000
    L=6: RQ(dir1, gen1..3) = +0.0000 +0.0000 +0.0000   RQ(all links, gen3) = -0.0000
    L=8: RQ(dir1, gen1..3) = +0.0000 +0.0000 +0.0000   RQ(all links, gen3) = -0.0000
  => those flat modes are EXACTLY zero at a centre-element configuration (the two
     occurrences of a direction-1 link in a plaquette carry the same right multiplier
     and U_1 = +-1 commutes with everything) => the corpus' description of the binding
     mode as 'uniform on the direction-1 links' is not correct; it is a staggering mode

[S7] AVERAGE vs SUP -- Gibbs measure at beta=2.5 (rough single-link Metropolis, L=2, [V])
  acceptance = 0.317   <d>_Gibbs = 5.1227 +- 0.0434
  Haar mean (L=2) = 4.5463 ; d_sup candidate >= 21.5753
  sup/average separation (densities) = 4.21x  -- same order, no exponential gap

[S8] TRACE vs OPERATOR NORM -- where the L^4 actually comes from
     tr(Cov) = E|grad S_W|^2 - |E grad S_W|^2 = THE NUMBER THE CORPUS QUOTES (dimension E*3)
     ||Cov||_op <= row sum  sum_{r,b} |Cov_{(0,a),(r,b)}|  (Gershgorin, block-Toeplitz)
     Bounding an operator norm by the trace is exactly a factor dim = E = 4 L^4.
  L     n_samp      tr(Cov)  tr(Cov)/N_p       rowsum    tr/rowsum
     2     160      442.493       4.6093       3.5064       126.20
       (16 shift classes used; the L=2 set is COMPLETE, L>=4 is a lower bound)
     4      64     7077.253       4.6076       4.3105      1641.86
       (81 shift classes used; the L=2 set is COMPLETE, L>=4 is a lower bound)
     6      32    35286.332       4.5379       3.4048     10363.64
       (81 shift classes used; the L=2 set is COMPLETE, L>=4 is a lower bound)
  => tr(Cov) grows like N_p = 6L^4 with a flat density (~4.6), while the row sum stays O(1)
     and L-independent.  ||Cov||_op = O(1);  the L^4 is the MATRIX DIMENSION E = 4L^4.

  And at the t = infinity endpoint the identity gives Cov_nu(grad S_W) = E_nu[Hess S_W]
  exactly, so ||Cov||_op <= sup_phi max_i rowsum_i(Hess S_W) = 6 beta = 15 is RIGOROUS
  (an average is bounded by the sup of the row sums, and the row sums are L-uniform).

DONE.
